Q: Making a flex item float right A: <p>You can't use <code>float</code> inside a <strong>flex container</strong> and the reason is that <strong>float property does not apply to flex-level boxes</strong> as you can see in <a href="https://jsfiddle.net/Lg0wyt9u/272/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this JSFiddle</a>.</p>
<p>So if you want to position the child element to right of the parent element you can use <code>margin-left: auto</code> but now the child element will also push other divs to the right as you can see in <a href="https://jsfiddle.net/Lg0wyt9u/273/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this JSFiddle</a>.</p>
<p>What you can do now is change order of elements and set <code>order: 2</code> on child element so it doesn't affect the second div:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="false" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.parent {
  display: flex;
}
.child {
  margin-left: auto;
  order: 2;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class="parent"&gt;
  &lt;div class="child"&gt;Ignore parent?&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;another child&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Inability to create a C file using C A: <p>Three things. First, when you compile your code, you should get errors (and warnings if enabled) that tell you why the code did not compile. When I compiled your code with gcc and default flags this was the error that showed up:</p>
<pre><code>test.c: In function ‘main’:
test.c:5:19: error: initialization of ‘FILE *’ from incompatible pointer type ‘char *’ [-Wincompatible-pointer-types]
    5 |     FILE *pTest = (&quot;output.c&quot;, &quot;w&quot;);
      |   
</code></pre>
<p>The compiler tells you the exact line where the issue occured. On line five you forgot to add the function name (fopen), so instead of calling fopen to open a file you have instead attempted to cast the char* &quot;output.c&quot; to a FILE*. Second, you're calling fprintf with the formatted string &quot;s&quot;, which will just print the character s to the file pTest, instead of printing the string to the file. When you want to print non literal characters using printf or fprintf, you need to use % followed by the corresponding sequence for your variable type (ex, %s = string, %d = int, %f = float, %lf = double). Finally, printf does not start a new line for you just like fprintf, so if you want your two print statements to be on their own lines, you should at a '\n' at the end. The corrected code looks like this:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main()
{
    FILE *pTest = fopen(&quot;output.c&quot;, &quot;w&quot;);

    if (pTest == NULL)
    {
        printf(&quot;Failed to create file!\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }

    //T = text
    char T1[] = &quot;#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n&quot;;
    char T2[] = &quot;int main()\n&quot;;
    char T3[] = &quot;{\n&quot;;
    char T4[] = &quot;printf(\&quot;Hello World!\&quot;);\n&quot;;
    char T5[] = &quot;return 0;\n&quot;;
    char T6[] = &quot;}\n&quot;;

    fprintf(pTest, &quot;%s&quot;, T1);
    fprintf(pTest, &quot;%s&quot;, T2);
    fprintf(pTest, &quot;%s&quot;, T3);
    fprintf(pTest, &quot;%s&quot;, T4);
    fprintf(pTest, &quot;%s&quot;, T5);
    fprintf(pTest, &quot;%s&quot;, T6);

    printf(&quot;File was created successfully!\n&quot;);

    fclose(pTest);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

